  "summary": "A group of landscape painters who worked in and around the village of Barbizon, France, part of a larger movement toward realism in art.",
  "description": "Characterized by detailed, realistic depictions of natural landscapes, particularly the forest of Fontainebleau. The artists rejected the artificiality of academic landscape painting, instead focusing on direct observation of nature. They often worked en plein air, capturing the effects of light and atmosphere with loose brushwork and natural color palettes.",
  "culturalContext": "The Barbizon School (1830-1870) — Rousseau, Corot, Millet, Daubigny — abandoned Paris for the Forest of Fontainebleau village to paint nature directly: oak interiors, peasant sowers, dusky cattle, paysage intime. Rebelling against Neoclassical landscape formulas, they made plein-air observation respectable a generation before Impressionism; Corot's silvery mornings and Millet's monumentality of peasants seeded both Impressionism and Van Gogh. The Barbizon aesthetic — muted gold-green tonalism, pastoral dignity — still defines \"forest mood\" painting.",
